WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
(playful music)

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.340
- Hi, my name is Sandrine Hazan

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.650
and I'm the Stranding and
Rehabilitation Manager

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.230
for the Sea Otter Program at
the Monterey Bay Aquarium.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.060
Come on in and take a sneak peek.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.370
The Otter Care Program at
the Monterey Bay Aquarium

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.580
takes in stranded, injured
and orphaned sea otters

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.210
to be rehabilitated and
released back into the wild.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.240
So, we actually spend
the majority of the time

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.700
observing these otters in this office,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.060
using our camera systems and the monitors.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.795
This is a great tool for us
to be able to observe them

00:00:35.795 --> 00:00:38.870
without directly impacting their behavior.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.050
These are the current otters
that we have in-house.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.230
And as you can tell the majority of them

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.520
have numbers for names.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.520
And this is so that we
don't become attached

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.370
to these otters that
are going to be released

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:49.220
back into the wild.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.160
Ivy is one of our resident
otters and she acts

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.740
as a surrogate for our orphaned pups.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.370
Like all wild animals, it's
important for the public

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.010
to give them their distance.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.140
Injured and orphaned otters can be very

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.790
dangerous and can bite.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.840
They can easily transmit
diseases to humans as well,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.150
which is why it's important
to keep your distance

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.190
and allow for experts to handle them.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.540
Paul is prepping some food.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.060
And what do we got?

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.560
We are currently feeding them, some squid.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.810
This is definitely one of
their least favorite items

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.110
but it's good for them to have a variety.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.000
Sea otters at the Monterey
Aquarium eat very well.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.660
It is important that
they have a diverse diet.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.040
Providing proper enrichment
is really important

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.490
for all age groups that are under our care

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.090
but specifically for pups.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.300
This gives them the
opportunity to practice

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.380
manipulating objects, which
then translates to being able

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.550
to handle live prey as they get older.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.410
Taking care of pups is a full-time job.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.610
They get offered formula
approximately every

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:49.680
three to four hours.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.130
And once they get older and
have the appropriate dentition,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.550
we then start offering solid food.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.180
Eventually we get them to the point

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.460
that they can recover food
and dive to the bottom

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.630
on their own, before
going in with a surrogate.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.860
One of our primary goals,
when working with these

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.840
dependent pups is ensuring
their successful release

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:09.673
back into the wild.

00:02:09.673 --> 00:02:11.490
And one of the ways
that we can do that, is

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.010
by helping to prevent imprinting
and habituation on humans.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.920
We wanna to make sure that
they don't associate anything

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.720
positive with humans,
such as food or grooming.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.730
In order to do that, we
need to disguise ourselves

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.260
to create a shapeless form,
that hides the human body.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.460
So, since this pup is
going to be released back

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.290
into the wild, I want to
make sure to disguise myself

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.330
properly, so that she's not
associating anything positive

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.642
with humans and warm formula is definitely

00:02:38.642 --> 00:02:40.170
something positive.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.440
So, I'm going to be wearing this disguise.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.240
First this, then a mask, cover my face,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.430
which I'll have down,
once I'm in with her.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.090
And then I also want a
way of protecting my arms

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.010
because like we talked about,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.437
these otters have sharp teeth.

00:02:54.437 --> 00:02:57.604
(bright guitar music)

00:03:03.425 --> 00:03:07.425
(bright guitar music continues)

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.430
Once a pup has been weaned
from their surrogate,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.110
they will spend more time in a tank

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.030
with different companion animals,
working on socialization,

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.440
continuing to learn how
to forage independently.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.630
And, at the point, in which we
see that they are consistent

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.050
in their behavior, body
condition looks good,

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.400
coat condition looks good.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.510
And around nine to 10
months is really the time

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.480
when we would start considering releasing.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.280
Given that we spend so much
time working with these otters

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.760
and rehabilitating them
and having gone through

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.090
the surrogacy program,
we want to make sure

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.470
that there is a way that we can track them

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.640
and monitor their progress,
once they're released

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.473
back into the wild.

00:03:51.473 --> 00:03:53.580
So, before they go, they are outfitted

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:54.991
with some of these accessories.

00:03:54.991 --> 00:03:57.800
The first thing that they
get is a radio transmitter,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.860
that is surgically
implanted in their abdomen.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.772
And this transmitter will
be able to emit a signal

00:04:02.772 --> 00:04:05.640
every time they're at
the surface of the water.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.736
And if we have an antenna and a receiver,

00:04:07.736 --> 00:04:11.070
we can find them and hear
them when they're foraging

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.680
or grooming at the surface of the water.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.210
The other thing that they
will get are flipper tags.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.320
Combination of two flipper
tags, one on each flipper,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.520
so that we can visually identify them

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.324
when they're out in the water.

00:04:22.324 --> 00:04:24.907
(gentle music)

00:04:33.448 --> 00:04:36.865
(gentle music continues)

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.940
Visit the Monterey Bay Aquarium's website,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.380
montereybayaquarium.org, to learn more

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.133
about how you can donate and help.

00:04:55.195 --> 00:04:57.778
